data.body:
@forteller while we wait for this enhancement, I highly suggest using https://microg.org/download.html.  If you build AOSP from source you just have to apply the patches @microg supplies to enable signature spoofing. Otherwise if you use Xposed (which defeats the purpose of ensuring privacy) you can install the signature spoofing module from there.  

microG Pro Tip: Use the latest preview release, otherwise apps that use the newer version of Google Play Services will complain about your Play Services being out of date. I try to keep a strictly FOSS device but find myself occassionally downloading proprietary developed or distributed software for temporary usage with @yeriomin's https://github.com/yeriomin/YalpStore (Slack for work, Juno, an Uber-like service currently only in NYC, and now Wire)

Off-topic:
@wireapp needs to make it a priority to get this client to the point where it can be submitted to F-Droid. Any usage of a Google's proprietary services (distribution or jars) negates any claim of full-privacy.  You have no idea what their library is doing and it's more than probably it is fingerprinting devices and calling home.

I had to install the mobile client due to conversation history "feature" which has caused lost messages because I proactively clear my browser's cookies, cache and storage.
